{
  "example": "{cite}`requirements.sdk.graph.evidence.persisted_dependencies`\n{cite}`requirements.sdk.graph.validation-context.snapshot`\n\nA first run assigns handle `7` to requirement `R` and records a query for its implementation edges. The next run assigns `7` to requirement `S` and `12` to `R`. Loading the record resolves its dependency to `12`. If `R` has the same implementation targets, their edge fingerprint still matches, even if their handles also changed. Removing an implementation target changes the fingerprint and makes the saved observation stale.",
  "determination": true,
  "rationale": "The example describes a concrete case: handles are reassigned between runs, the saved dependency resolves to the new handle, the edge fingerprint still matches when the targets are the same, and it goes stale when an implementation target is removed. The one test, `crates/trust-but-verify/tests/contracts.rs#fn[saved_node_and_edge_inputs_survive_a_different_handle_allocation_order]`, is marked `{test}` for the example. It covers each outcome:\n\n- **Reassigned handles.** The first domain interns the source `Node(1)` and then the target `Node(2)`. The fresh domain, with an empty cache, interns `Node(2)` first and then the descriptor source. That swaps the two handles. The test asserts `fresh.cache().node_data(subject).is_none()` and `assert_ne!(fresh_target, target)`.\n- **Loading resolves to the new handle.** `validator.load_evidence(&descriptor)` returns dependencies matching `[Dependency::Node(n, _), Dependency::OutgoingEdges(s, 7, _)]`. There `n == fresh_target` and `s == descriptor.source`, so the saved data resolves to the fresh domain's handles. This is the `7` to `12` case.\n- **Dependency kind, label and fingerprint preserved.** `durable_inputs` converts the saved and loaded dependencies back to `Node` data. The test asserts this equals the originally persisted `expected`. That covers `persisted_dependencies`: kind, label `7`, and fingerprint all survive.\n- **Same targets still match despite changed handles.** `saved.is_current(&fresh, ...)` and `validator.check_inputs(&loaded)` both return true. The edge fingerprint is recomputed over the target's new handle. The target is still `Node(2)`, so it keeps its stable path.\n- **Removing a target makes the observation stale.** `fresh.0.lock().unwrap().edges.clear()` removes the implementation target, and `check_inputs` then returns false. The node contents are unchanged, so the staleness comes from the edge fingerprint.\n\nThe test only removes a sole target, which gives an empty set. That is a removal, and the example names no other case. The domain's `node_path` derives stable paths from node data, not handles, so the expectations come from the requirement text and not from the implementation. I found no gap.",
  "references": "9bdb5d2359ecc6b6aa9f53587247fcf20540afa029dc6fa82abff9bd40fe9472",
